TI  - Impact of Treatment Outcome on Mortality in Mycobacterium abscessus Complex 
      Pulmonary Disease.
PG  - 349-359
LID - S0012-3692(25)05175-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2025.07.4112 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Mycobacterium abscessus complex (MABC) is a major causative pathogen 
      of nontuberculous mycobacterial pulmonary disease (PD) and is notoriously 
      difficult to treat. However, the impact of culture conversion on survival has not 
      been explored in previous studies. Herein, we investigated the relationship 
      between culture conversion and mortality in patients with MABC-PD. RESEARCH 
      QUESTION: Is culture conversion associated with mortality in patients with 
      MABC-PD? STUDY DESIGN AND METHODS: We conducted a retrospective study analyzing 
      patients with MABC-PD who completed at least 6 months of macrolide-based 
      treatment at a tertiary referral center in South Korea from 2012 to 2023. We 
      assessed the association between culture conversion and all-cause mortality 
      during the follow-up period using extended Cox proportional-hazards models, with 
      culture conversion treated as a time-dependent covariate. RESULTS: Among the 172 
      patients, 100 (58.1%) achieved culture conversion at treatment completion, 
      whereas 72 (41.9%) did not. The median follow-up period was 4.4 (interquartile 
      range, 2.5-7.9) and 3.1 (interquartile range, 1.9-4.8) years for patients with 
      and without culture conversion, respectively. The all-cause mortality rate was 
      significantly higher in patients without culture conversion (22.5% vs 6.0%; P = 
      .002). The 5- and 10-year mortality rates were 5.5% and 7.7% among patients who 
      achieved culture conversion, compared with 25.7% and 51.0% among those who did 
      not, respectively. Cox regression analysis revealed a 1.57-fold increased 
      mortality risk in patients without culture conversion (95% CI, 1.13-2.20; P = 
      .008). INTERPRETATION: This study identifies culture conversion at treatment 
      completion as a key prognostic factor in patients with MABC-PD, with failure to 
      achieve conversion significantly associated with increased mortality. Given the 
      poor treatment outcomes of MABC-PD, novel therapeutic strategies are needed to 
      enhance culture conversion and improve long-term prognosis.
