OWN - NLM
STAT- Publisher
LR  - 20260622
IS  - 1931-3543 (Electronic)
IS  - 0012-3692 (Linking)
DP  - 2026 May 22
TI  - Geography as a Determinant of Donor Access for Lung Transplantation in the United 
      States.
LID - S0012-3692(26)00652-5 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2026.05.019 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Geographic equity in access to donor lungs is a central objective of 
      US organ allocation policy. In March 2023, lung allocation transitioned from 
      rigid donor-candidate distance thresholds to a continuous distribution framework 
      using the Composite Allocation Score (CAS). The CAS prioritizes medical urgency, 
      projected posttransplant survival, biological compatibility, and geographic 
      efficiency (continuous donor-candidate distance). On May 7, 2026, a policy 
      amendment was approved that increased the influence of proximity on candidate 
      ranking. RESEARCH QUESTION: Is listing transplant center location associated with 
      effective donor availability, and how will the amended CAS policy affect 
      candidate prioritization? STUDY DESIGN AND METHODS: We conducted a retrospective 
      cohort study using Scientific Registry of Transplant Recipients data including 
      adult lung transplant candidates, recipients, and donors (September 27, 
      2023-December 1, 2024). The primary outcome was distance-adjusted donor 
      availability, defined as biologically compatible donors recovered within 30 days 
      of listing, weighted by the CAS geographic efficiency function. Spatial 
      regression evaluated geographic variation across transplant centers. 
      Counterfactual analyses simulated allocation under the CAS amendment. Match-run 
      analyses assessed changes in candidate prioritization for donor organs recovered 
      during the study period. RESULTS: Among 3,917 candidates from 61 centers, median 
      center-level distance-adjusted donor availability was 1.90 donors per 
      candidate-day (interquartile range, 1.75-2.14), with substantial variation across 
      centers and regions. After adjustment for center size and candidate biological 
      characteristics, location remained significantly associated with donor 
      availability. The amended policy would reduce effective donor availability and 
      increase geographic disparities in donor access. In match-run analyses, 23.9% of 
      candidates ranked within the top 10 under the prior CAS policy fell outside the 
      top 10 under the amended CAS policy. Candidates with biological barriers to donor 
      matching, including blood type O, experienced greater deprioritization. 
      INTERPRETATION: Our results show that geographic variation in effective donor 
      availability persists under CAS policies. Increasing the influence of 
      donor-candidate proximity may increase geographic disparities in donor access, 
      particularly for harder-to-match candidates.
