IS  - 0012-3692 (Linking)
DP  - 2025 Dec 18
TI  - Can Telemonitoring Predict Patient-Reported Leak Perception in Long-Term Home 
      Noninvasive Ventilation for Patients with Chronic Respiratory Failure?
LID - S0012-3692(25)05929-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2025.11.050 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Home noninvasive ventilation (NIV) improves outcomes in patients with 
      chronic respiratory failure; however, telemonitoring remains challenging due to 
      undefined appropriate alert thresholds. Current leak thresholds lack clinical 
      validation, potentially leading to inefficient care delivery and alert fatigue 
      among health care providers. RESEARCH QUESTION: Can telemonitoring leak data 
      predict patient-reported perceptions of leaks in home NIV for patients with 
      chronic respiratory failure? STUDY DESIGN AND METHODS: Data collected between 
      March 2019 and May 2023 from 571 patients with chronic respiratory failure 
      receiving home NIV were analyzed retrospectively. Inclusion required at least 1 
      S3-NIV questionnaire and 28 days of preceding telemonitoring leak data. 
      Twenty-seven leak features were extracted across 7-, 14-, 21-, and 28-day 
      windows. Statistical analysis included analyses of variance, effect size 
      calculation (eta squared; η(2)), and comparison of predictive performance using 
      linear mixed-effects regression and K-nearest neighbors algorithms. RESULTS: Only 
      S3-NIV item 8 ("I am disturbed by leaks") showed significant associations with 
      leak features. Whereas no demographic or NIV setting differences were found 
      between patients reporting leak disturbances and those who did not, disturbed 
      patients consistently reported more NIV-related issues and respiratory symptoms. 
      Leak features reflecting central tendency and magnitude showed the highest effect 
      sizes (η(2) = 0.021-0.075). However, predictive models demonstrated limited 
      ability to accurately predict patient-perceived leaks. INTERPRETATION: 
      Telemonitoring leak data alone have limitations in predicting patient perception 
      of leaks, suggesting that individual factors beyond objective measurements play a 
      significant role. A proactive patient-centered management approach integrating 
      telemonitoring data and patient-reported outcomes is thus needed. CLINICAL TRIAL 
      REGISTRATION: Registered on Health Data Hub; No. 23517022; URL: 
      www.health-data-hub.fr.
