DP  - 2026 Jul
TI  - Association of Inhaling Marijuana With Asthma Attacks: An Analysis of Nationally 
      Representative Survey Data.
PG  - 32-40
LID - S0012-3692(26)00324-7 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2026.03.004 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Marijuana has been increasing in popularity. Asthma is an 
      inflammatory airway disease that can cause a range of respiratory symptoms. 
      Currently, there are extremely limited studies on the effects of marijuana use on 
      asthma attacks. RESEARCH QUESTION: What is the association between inhaling 
      marijuana and asthma attacks in US adults with asthma? STUDY DESIGN AND METHODS: 
      Waves 4 to 6 of the Population Assessment of Tobacco and Health (PATH) study, a 
      longitudinal study examining tobacco use and its impact on various aspects of 
      health, were used for this analysis. The presence of asthma attacks at follow-up 
      waves was compared between individuals who did not use marijuana and those who 
      inhaled combustible marijuana, inhaled electronically delivered marijuana, or 
      used both at their baseline wave (dichotomous variables). Presence of asthma 
      attacks was defined by self-report use of oral or injected corticosteroid to 
      treat an attack in the past 12 months. Statistical analysis was conducted by 
      using a weighted multivariable logistic regression model adjusting for baseline 
      characteristics. RESULTS: In this study involving 4,477 participants, inhaling 
      marijuana was significantly linked to higher rates of asthma exacerbations 1 year 
      from baseline. Compared with those who do not use marijuana, marijuana 
      participants who inhaled combustible marijuana products only (smoke; OR, 1.64; 
      95% CI, 1.12-2.40; P < .05), electronic products only (vape; OR, 1.81; 95% CI, 
      1.06-3.08; P < .05), or both combustible and electronic products (smoke and vape; 
      OR, 1.57; 95% CI, 1.01-2.45; P < .05) had greater odds of reporting an asthma 
      attack in the subsequent wave. INTERPRETATION: Our results show inhaling 
      marijuana was significantly linked to a higher risk of asthma attacks from 1 year 
      baseline. Given the increasing use of marijuana among young adults, more research 
      is needed to better understand its long-term effects on respiratory health, 
      especially asthma outcomes.
