PMID- 41921908
OWN - NLM
STAT- Publisher
LR  - 20260506
IS  - 1931-3543 (Electronic)
IS  - 0012-3692 (Linking)
DP  - 2026 Mar 30
TI  - Effects of a Sea-Level Stay on Pulmonary Hemodynamics in Patients With Pulmonary 
      Arterial Hypertension Living in Quito Near 2,850 m.
LID - S0012-3692(26)00423-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2026.02.043 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Among the > 80 million people living > 2,500 m are patients with 
      pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH). RESEARCH QUESTION: How does descent to sea 
      level (SL) affect pulmonary hemodynamics and 6-minute walk distance (6MWD) in 
      patients with PAH living at high altitude (HA)? STUDY DESIGN AND METHODS: HA 
      residents with PAH were transported to SL. Vital signs including heart rate (HR), 
      arterial oxygen saturation (Sao(2)), 6MWD, systolic pulmonary artery pressure 
      (sPAP), cardiac output (CO), and other measures were assessed at 2,850 m and 
      after the first night (day 2) and second night (day 3) at SL. RESULTS: 
      Twenty-eight patients (24 female; age, 46 ± 12 y), treated with phosphodiesterase 
      type 5 inhibitors (n = 28) and endothelin receptor antagonists (n = 9), were 
      included. Sao(2), sPAP, HR, CO, and 6MWD at HA were 90% ± 5%, 68 ± 24 mm Hg, 74 ± 
      14 bpm, 3.9 ± 1.1 L/min, and 462 ± 100 m. On SL day 2, Sao(2) was higher (+5.5% 
      [3.9%-7.1%], sPAP and HR were lower (-11 [-16 to -5] mm Hg and -7 [-12 to -3] 
      bpm) (all P < .001), resulting in unchanged sPAP/CO and oxygen delivery. HR and 
      CO further decreased on day 3, resulting in unchanged sPAP/CO vs HA. 6MWD was 
      higher at SL vs HA on day 2 (+41 [16-67] m; P < .001) but not day 3. No adverse 
      events occurred, including after re-ascent. INTERPRETATION: Our results show that 
      typical patients with PAH living at 2,850 m reveal improved blood oxygenation 
      after descent to SL along with decreased sPAP, but also HR, resulting in 
      unchanged pulmonary resistance and oxygen delivery. Although 6MWD initially 
      increased at SL, it returned to high-altitude baseline after the second night, 
      along with further HR and CO decrease. CLINICAL TRIAL REGISTRATION: 
      ClinicalTrials.gov; Nos.: NCT06092424, NCT05996302; URL: www. CLINICALTRIALS: 
      gov.
