TI  - A 38-Year-Old Woman With Sudden Dyspnea After Uterine Artery Embolization.
PG  - e11-e16
LID - S0012-3692(26)00147-9 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2026.01.020 [doi]
AB  - A 38-year-old woman (gravida and para G4P3003) at 31 weeks' gestation with a 
      history of placenta increta, hypothyroidism, and 3 prior cesarean deliveries 
      sought treatment for vaginal bleeding. She reported occasional cramping and 
      normal fetal movement, but did not report dizziness, chest pain, dyspnea, or 
      urinary symptoms. She was found to have recurrent placenta increta, and after a 
      multidisciplinary discussion, she was admitted for cesarean delivery with planned 
      hysterectomy within 1 week of delivery. During the postpartum phase, her 
      hemoglobin level continued to decline, despite blood transfusions. She 
      subsequently underwent uterine artery embolization using Avitene or Embosphere, 
      Gelfoam, glue, and coils before the hysterectomy. During the procedure, she 
      demonstrated sinus tachycardia (120-130 beats/min) and required supplemental 
      oxygen (4 L/min). The pulmonary team was consulted for worsening hypoxia.
