TI  - A 28-Year-Old Man With Consecutive Gastrointestinal Symptoms, Kidney Failure, and 
      Progressive Neurologic Deterioration.
PG  - e141-e144
LID - S0012-3692(25)00681-6 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2025.05.031 [doi]
AB  - A 28-year-old man with an unremarkable medical history, no regular medication 
      regimen, and no recent travel history or animal exposure was admitted to the 
      emergency department with new onset of disorientation, slurred speech, and 
      hearing loss. One day prior, the patient had consulted his general practitioner 
      for fever, recurrent vomiting, and myalgia. According to his family, the patient 
      smoked cigarettes and daily ingested γ-hydroxybutyrate. Because of the patient's 
      limited ability to communicate, recent ingestion of other substances could not be 
      ruled out. The patient was admitted to the ICU because of severe metabolic 
      acidosis, electrolyte imbalances, and acute renal failure of indeterminate 
      origin. Hemodialysis was started right after admission.
