TI  - A 71-Year-Old Man With Neck Pain, Ptosis, and Complex Cystic Superior Mediastinal 
      Lesion.
PG  - e123-e127
LID - S0012-3692(25)05622-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2025.09.137 [doi]
AB  - A 71-year-old man sought treatment at the emergency department with a 3-week 
      history of posterior neck pain, generalized weakness, and new-onset right-sided 
      ptosis. He described the neck pain as sharp (6 out of 10) and nonradiating, with 
      a sensation of neck "dropping" that required intermittent manual repositioning 
      and support with a neck brace. Pertinent positive findings included increased 
      fatigue, dizziness, loss of balance, and excessive sleep. Pertinent negative 
      findings included recent trauma, travel, sick contacts, chest pain, shortness of 
      breath, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, altered bowel or urinary habits, 
      headache, and fever. His past medical history included dyslipidemia, 
      nephrolithiasis, and benign prostatic hyperplasia. He reported that he does not 
      smoke or consume alcohol and has no occupational exposure risks.
