DP  - 2026 May 12
TI  - Pretreatment With Mono or Dual Pulmonary Hypertension-Targeted Medical Therapy in 
      Patients Undergoing Balloon Pulmonary Angioplasty: Effect on Complications and 
      Clinical Outcomes.
LID - S0012-3692(26)00624-0 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2026.04.048 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Pretreatment with pulmonary hypertension (PH)-targeted medical 
      therapy has been shown to reduce balloon pulmonary angioplasty (BPA) 
      complications in patients with chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension 
      (CTEPH). Data collected in the Dutch BPA registry allowed for comparison between 
      mono and dual PH-targeted medical pretreatment in patients undergoing BPA. 
      RESEARCH QUESTION: Does pretreatment with dual PH-targeted medical therapy lead 
      to fewer BPA complications and improved clinical outcomes in comparison with 
      monotherapy? STUDY DESIGN AND METHODS: This study included all consecutive 
      patients with CTEPH who underwent BPA after pretreatment with either mono or dual 
      therapy in The Netherlands. Periprocedural BPA complications were assessed in 
      addition to treatment strategy, clinical parameters, and pulmonary hemodynamics 
      at diagnosis, first BPA, and 6-month follow-up. RESULTS: A total of 169 patients 
      with CTEPH (53% female; mean age ± SD, 66 ± 12 years; 65% World Health 
      Organization functional class III/IV; mean pulmonary artery pressure ± SD, 44 ± 
      12 mm Hg; pulmonary vascular resistance [PVR] ± SD, 7.7 ± 4.3 Wood units) were 
      included. Of the patients, 93 (55%) received pretreatment with monotherapy and 76 
      (45%) received pretreatment with dual PH-targeted medical therapy. On a per-BPA 
      session basis, patients pretreated with dual therapy experienced fewer 
      BPA-related thoracic complications (8.4% vs 19.6%; P < .001) than those 
      pretreated with monotherapy. When corrected for center, age, PVR and multiple BPA 
      sessions per patient, independent predictors of thoracic BPA complications were 
      higher logarithmic N-terminal prohormone brain natriuretic peptide (OR, 1.40; 95% 
      CI, 1.07-1.83; P = .01), higher right atrial pressure (OR, 1.09; 95% CI, 
      1.02-1.17; P = .01), and mono PH-targeted medical pretreatment (OR, 3.33; 95% CI, 
      1.43-7.69; P = .005). Between baseline and 6-month follow-up, patients pretreated 
      with dual PH-target medical therapy showed significantly greater improvement in 
      6-minute walking distance, cardiac index, and PVR. INTERPRETATION: This study 
      demonstrates that pretreatment with dual PH-targeted medical therapy was 
      associated with lower (thoracic) periprocedural BPA complications rates. 
      Additionally, clinical outcomes were significantly better in those pretreated 
      with dual PH-targeted medical therapy compared with monotherapy.
