TI  - A 62-Year-Old-Man With Severe Daytime Sleepiness After Primary Central Nervous 
      System Lymphoma.
PG  - e55-e60
LID - S0012-3692(25)05149-9 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2025.08.017 [doi]
AB  - A 62-year-old man with a history of primary central nervous system lymphoma first 
      presented with diplopia, diagnosed 2 years ago. A brain MRI showed multiple solid 
      enhancing mass lesions located at the midline bilateral dorsal medulla to both 
      cervicomedullary junction and right flocculus with edema of the ventrolateral 
      medulla. The brain biopsy confirmed relapsed diffuse large B cell lymphoma. 
      Remission was achieved after a course of high-dose methotrexate, rituximab, and 
      whole-brain radiotherapy. He was referred to the sleep clinic because of snoring, 
      witnessed apnea, and severe daytime sleepiness. He had no dyspnea, cough, or 
      history of aspiration pneumonia. Medications included levetiracetam and 
      midodrine.
