TI  - High-Flow Nasal Cannula Liberation: Who, When, and How?
PG  - 1152-1161
LID - S0012-3692(25)00155-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2025.01.037 [doi]
AB  - High-flow nasal cannula (HFNC) is a first-line therapy for patients with acute 
      respiratory failure. Despite increased HFNC utilization over recent 
      years-accelerated in part by the COVID-19 pandemic-high-quality evidence to guide 
      HFNC discontinuation is lacking. Decisions about when and how quickly to reduce 
      flow rate, Fio(2), or both simultaneously are frequently left to clinicians' 
      discretion without clear guidance on an optimal approach. Failure to de-escalate 
      HFNC support when clinically appropriate has many potential consequences, such as 
      prolongation of ICU/hospital length of stay, increased health care costs, and 
      reduced availability of limited hospital resources. With the goal of improving 
      care efficiency and resource utilization among hospitalized patients with acute 
      respiratory failure, we propose a standardized approach for HFNC discontinuation 
      focused on "liberation" (similar to spontaneous breathing trials for patients 
      undergoing mechanical ventilation), using a stepwise approach guided by 
      physiology.
