TI  - Timely Primary Care Follow-Up Following Hospitalization for Pediatric Critical 
      Illness.
PG  - 652-664
LID - S0012-3692(25)05256-0 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2025.08.036 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Timely follow-up after PICU hospitalization provides an important 
      opportunity to support recovery following critical illness. However, the limited 
      available data suggest that timely follow-up does not occur regularly. RESEARCH 
      QUESTION: What are the rate of and factors associated with timely follow-up with 
      primary and/or specialty care clinicians following PICU hospitalization? STUDY 
      DESIGN AND METHODS: Using the Merative MarketScan Commercial Database and the 
      Transformed Medicaid Statistical Information System Analytic Files, this study 
      identified PICU hospitalizations for publicly and privately insured children (age 
      0-18 years) with a PICU hospitalization from January 2016 to December 2019. 
      RESULTS: Primary and/or subspecialty visits within 7 days of PICU hospitalization 
      discharge were analyzed. Multivariable logistic regression was used to report the 
      adjusted probability of timely follow-up according to patient and hospitalization 
      characteristics. Among 333,920 and 41,223 eligible PICU hospitalizations for 
      publicly and privately insured children, 149,028 (44.6%) and 19,037 (46.2%) were 
      for female participants and 178,295 (53.4%) and 27,294 (66.2%) were for children 
      with a complex chronic condition, respectively. In the 7 days after discharge 
      from PICU hospitalization, 82,009 (24.6%) and 12,299 (29.8%) of hospitalizations 
      for publicly and privately insured children had primary care follow-up within 7 
      days of PICU hospitalization discharge; 29,623 (8.9%) and 4,099 (9.9%) had 
      subspecialty follow-up, and 102,631 (30.7%) and 15,412 (37.4%) had any outpatient 
      follow-up. In adjusted analyses, younger age and lack of complex chronic 
      conditions were associated with a higher likelihood of primary care follow-up. 
      INTERPRETATION: In this US cohort of > 375,000 critically ill children with 
      public or private insurance, less than one-third had primary care follow-up 
      within 1 week of hospital discharge. Younger children and children without 
      chronic conditions were more likely to have primary care follow-up. Children who 
      survive PICU hospitalization may benefit from specific follow-up recommendations 
      to ensure continuity in care.
